{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 20,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the basic sequence: Pinel completed a temporary mortgage workout payment plan, Aurora foreclosed, and Pinel opposed dismissal of her contract claim."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The listed defects concern ordinary whole-contract interpretation of repayment and forbearance promises, not the technical California foreclosure rules discussed elsewhere in the opinion."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the ambiguity requires comparing the repayment recital, paragraph 3's forbearance promise, Attachment A's payment-purpose language, and Attachment A(b)'s insufficiency disclosure."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Opinion lines 52–54 plainly identify the competing readings and explain that the apparent contradiction prevents dismissal without deciding Aurora's ultimate repayment obligation."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "All listed defects arise from the same relational ambiguity, so the reviewer must distinguish each provision's role rather than treat them as independent drafting problems."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02: This defect concerns Aurora's claim that forbearance was its only obligation, rather than an ambiguity within paragraph 3 itself. The reviewer must connect that limitation argument to the repayment provisions and recognize that the court rejected resolving it at the pleading stage, not the forbearance promise itself.",
  "reason": "A general contract reviewer can handle this case with a focused comparison of the relevant provisions and opinion lines 52–54. The principal challenge is separating the provision-specific descriptions of one shared ambiguity, especially the argument that temporary forbearance exhausted Aurora's obligations. The reviewer must also distinguish the court's finding of apparent ambiguity from a final holding that Aurora promised a particular cure opportunity or loan modification. No specialist foreclosure research or detailed review of the UCL analysis is needed to understand the listed defects."
}
